title: 'eth-rpc: read block data from storage, rate-limit backfill, tune health check'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    ### Problem
    eth-rpc falls behind the chain head and its pod gets restarted (see paritytech/devops#5224), with the following observations:
    - eth-rpc makes many runtime API calls
    - the node runs out of Wasm instances
    - the eth-rpc runtime API calls get slow (tens of seconds)
    - backward sync adds extra load on the node
    - the health check reports unhealthy even on a small lag, and enough consecutive failures restart the pod

    ### Change
    1. Reduce the runtime API calls eth-rpc makes by reading the Ethereum block, block hash, and receipt data directly from storage instead of via `state_call`. These are plain storage items, so a storage read returns the same values without spawning the runtime.
    2. Rate-limit historic backfill: add `--backward-sync-max-blocks-per-sec` (default `30`, `0` disables) to cap how fast the backward sync reads blocks, so backfill does not overload the node.
    3. Tune the health check:
       - Bound the node query with a 3s timeout: if the node does not answer in time, log a warning instead of letting the probe silently time out.
       - Report unhealthy only when eth-rpc is more than 128 blocks behind the node, to account for node block import burstiness.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
